PTY(4)                         P32W Unix                          PTY(4)

NAME
       pty - pseudo-terminals

SYNOPSIS
       /dev/ptyp0-15  masters: character device 6, minors 0-15
       /dev/ttyp0-15  slaves: character device 1, minors 5-20

DESCRIPTION
       A pseudo-terminal is a terminal with a program at the other end
       of the line instead of a keyboard and screen. The slave,
       /dev/ttypN, behaves like tty0: echo, line editing, ^C and the
       other signals, CBREAK and RAW modes (ioctl TIOCSETP), and it
       becomes the controlling terminal of whoever opens it first, so
       getty, login, the shell and vi work on it unchanged.

       The master, /dev/ptypN, is the other end. What is written to it
       arrives as the slave's typing; reading it gives what the slave's
       programs have written (with CR LF, as a terminal gets).
       select(2) works on both.

       A master can be open once at a time, and only when its slave is
       not still open from before; a slave can be opened only while
       its master is. Closing the master hangs up the slave: the
       processes it controls get SIGHUP, and their reads then end. A
       read of the master ends (returns 0) once the slave has been
       opened and closed again by everyone: the session is over.

       The master may say what size the terminal is, and ask it:
       ioctl(2)'s TIOCSWINSZ and TIOCGWINSZ on the master are the
       slave's. A new master's terminal has no size (0 by 0). The
       modes are the slave's alone to set.

       telnetd(8) uses pairs 0 to 3, which change their owner at a
       login; wm(1) uses 4 to 15, a window each, which only root
       and group staff may open.

FILES
       /dev/ptyp0-15, /dev/ttyp0-15

SEE ALSO
       telnetd(8), wm(1), getty(8), stty(1), ioctl(2)

BUGS
       Sixteen pairs. telnetd does not ask the caller's terminal
       for its size: over the network it is not known until
       stty(1) says it.
